{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.AveragedCrossingCollapse","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.AveragedCrossingCollapse","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":7,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.cube_eq_scalar_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.cube_eq_scalar_mul","kind":"theorem","line":101,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/AveragedCrossingCollapse.lean#L101-L108","doc":"**Saturation.**  A third averaged crossing on the same generator\nreintroduces `σ`: the cube is the collapse scalar times `(σ + d)`, so no\nsecond `(q²+1)²` is produced.  This is why the measured cancellation is 2 even\nwhen a generator carries three averaged crossings. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.scaledCollapseScalar_eq_cyclotomicSq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.scaledCollapseScalar_eq_cyclotomicSq","kind":"theorem","line":93,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/AveragedCrossingCollapse.lean#L93-L99","doc":"**The scalar is the cyclotomic square.**  With `h = q - q⁻¹` — carried\ndivision-free as `q * h = q² - 1` — the collapse scalar scaled by `4q²` is\nexactly `(q² + 1)²`, the factor whose cancellation the Q2 measurement\nobserved, once per pair of co-located averaged crossings. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.four_sq_add_sub_one_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.four_sq_add_sub_one_sq","kind":"theorem","line":88,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/AveragedCrossingCollapse.lean#L88-L91","doc":"The bare cyclotomic identity behind the collapse scalar. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.sigmaCoeff_identity_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.sigmaCoeff_identity_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":82,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/AveragedCrossingCollapse.lean#L82-L86","doc":"**Control.**  An identity crossing `d = 0` leaves `σ` coefficient `h`,\nagain nonzero whenever `h` is — no collapse. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.sigmaCoeff_full_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.sigmaCoeff_full_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":75,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/AveragedCrossingCollapse.lean#L75-L80","doc":"**Control.**  A full crossing `d = -h` leaves `σ` coefficient `-h`, nonzero\nwhenever `h` is — no collapse. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.sq_sigma_coeff_vanishes","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.sq_sigma_coeff_vanishes","kind":"theorem","line":65,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/AveragedCrossingCollapse.lean#L65-L73","doc":"**The collapse.**  For the colour-averaged coefficient, characterised\ndivision-free by `2 * d = -h`, the `σ` coefficient vanishes and the square of\nan averaged crossing is the scalar `1 + d²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.sq_sigma_coeff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.sq_sigma_coeff","kind":"theorem","line":59,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/AveragedCrossingCollapse.lean#L59-L63","doc":"The `σ` coefficient of `(σ + d)²` under the Hecke relation\n`σ² = h·σ + 1`, before any choice of `d`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}